i'm now joined by anakin fitfeld the  norwegian foreign minister foreign  minister ukraine has been launching  quite a successful counter-offensive in  recent days how much is norway prepared  to deliver to ukraine so that this  momentum can actually be seized  prime minister sturrid visited the kiev  this summer  and promised  funding to ukraine for like 1 billion  euro which i think will be important  this money goes to weapons of course  but also to humanitarian assistance and  also money so they can  buy more gas  so it's important for us to unite with  ukraine  and no way we haven't delivered weapons  to to counter countries in conflict for  more than 60 years but we changed the  practice when this war started  like countries like sweden and finland  also did so  there's a enormous solidarity  with the ukrainian people  in norway we do also have a border to  russia so we know what this neighbor is  about even though they have a different  policy up in the high north  than they have towards ukraine  so changing this position of actually  delivering weapons to a crisis area that  is of course something that you have in  common with a country like germany we've  also changed our position here in this  country  what should germany be doing which  weapons should it deliver to ukraine  well it's a purely german decision  so  that's my message  but as an overall view  i think it has been very important that  western countries have been sending  weapons to ukraine so they can be able  to protect themselves  without those weapons there would be  another game in another situation in  ukraine right now norway also supports  ukraine in terms of delivering  humanitarian aid now you've come to  berlin to discuss the topic of gender  issues in foreign policy  so which role do gender issues play in  this conflict with russia we get several  reports about  how women are affected on the ground we  can see rapes used as a weapon in this  war and also other wars all around the  world so we need to focus very clearly  on the consequences for both men and  women in this conflict  and more broadly perhaps gender issues  in foreign policy is something that  norway has long advocated for but it is  a topic that not many countries so far  have been taking on board that is  beginning to change  and why are the scandinavian countries  such an exception and what can other  countries learn from your country  we have this experience that if we are  bringing women  to the table  in a conflict situation  the peace deal tend to last longer  basically because their perspectives are  being brought in  we see that in several conflicts in the  world  we have hosted  a dialogue between  the different groups in colombia for  like 10 years to get with cuba  and there has been  very many women  led delegations women have been included  around the table  as victims but also as mediators so  that's absolutely crucial in order to  let peace still last  norway supports  multilateralism as an approach to  essentially look at solutions for  worldwide conflicts now multilateralism  is being tested in the arctic of course  where melting ice is now bringing more  natural resources to the surface and  countries like the us and china and  russia are using this field to pursue  their own strategic interests how  uncomfortable is this for norway  well i think it's a general description  of the situation  that there is more like status quo in  the arctic  we are trying to do our atmos to keep  low tension  in the high north but we can really see  very visible the climate changes  up there  and we need to cooperate very strongly  with the other arctic nations  norway has of course been a nato member  from the very start of the alliance now  finland and sweden also in scandinavia  are about to join the alliance what  hopes do you have for their membership  it means a lot to our security because  we have been working stronger and  stronger together with finland and  sweden over the last  years  after 1989 but still there has been have  been limitations due to the fact that  they have been  neutral and we have been a member of  nato but now it's very much easier  to build a common defense strategy that  will make norway safer  the nato alliance has given itself this  goal of spending two percent of the  national budget  on defence  now what is the discussion in norway  right now do people feel like more is  necessary  yeah we do have a program now for  strengthening  our military presence up in the high  north we have a very special security  policy  because we  base our  security policy on the churns  together with allies so we host a huge  military exercise  earlier this spring with thousands of  soldiers from  close allies around europe and also  canada and  united states came to the norwegian  waters and took took  that took place in  in nowhere this spring but on the other  hand we also trying to do our atmos to  keep low tension  and by doing that  we are balancing norwegian interest up  in the high north and which lessons can  be drawn from russia's war on ukraine  for other crises around the world  we've been in the security council for  almost two years now  and we have been able even in this  situation to find common solutions  by working very closely  with other countries in the security  council and this is very important that  we don't get a spill over effect to  other conflicts i mean some conflicts  will be more difficult to solve  but others  may be a window of opportunity because  this conflict this war has global  consequences  uh we can see food increase in food  prices increase in energy prices  however we need to maintain the  architecture as good as we can but it's  difficult to work with russia now of  course  when you look at other crises around the  world which are the crises that you have  your eye on most  we are working strongly  on colombia and also venezuela  i am going to share a meeting in a donor  group for palestine  when i'm going to new york next week  and a new situation in the middle east  where we need to encourage  more dialogue between  all the countries in that region  will also be a priority  for norway so we have been taking part  in many talks  during the last  30 years however we never put pressure  on the parties that's not the norwegian  model  but we will be happy if  countries or parts in a conflict  would invite norway as a mediator in  several of these conflicts  here in europe many people for decades  have relied on the fact that the us  would ultimately protect europe and that  european countries don't need to be too  active themselves but we've seen the  united states be more and more focused  on the indo-pacific so what should a  country like germany which is of course  one of europe's strongest countries  do in terms of protecting europe  well that's actually my point when i'm  giving speeches about the new security  there will be less attention from united  states because they have their eyes on  what's happening in in asia and that  part of the world and we need to take  major core  care of our own territories and germany  certainly are stepping up  and we've had a very very strong  cooperation with germany during the last  years and this will be strengthened  strengthened i think in the years to  come  would your country consider joining the  european union there are different  opinions on that in  in no way we had one referendum in 72  where people voted no and another one in  94 where also the majority of people  voted no  but there is  ongoing debate in a way in society  whether we should join or not anakin  whitfeld norwegian foreign minister  thank you very much  you
 

